<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shepenupet I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shepenupet I was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-iii">Osorkon III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun">God's Wife of Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> in the 8th century BCE, the first holder of the office to wield wide political power. Under Kushite pressure she adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenirdis-i">Amenirdis I</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a>, as her successor, handing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushites</a> a peaceful hold on Thebes. She appears on the chapel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> Heqadjet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, built by her father and brother.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shepenupet-i.jpg" alt="Shepenupet I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neithsabes · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karnak_Chepenoupet_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
